1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
It was really fortunate I got down to camp again this week just to, you know, look around, talk to some of the counselors, talk to the

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Orienteering Counselor who I really like. He has a very magnetic personality.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Even though he can be a little overbearing at times, I declined to needle him about all that.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Hey, this is podcast number 74.

6
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Hey, welcome back to the scout master podcast. This is Clark Green.

7
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So, uh, yeah, sorry about that there at the beginning puns, more puns.

8
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
You didn't know there were that many scouting puns, did you?

9
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
No, my goodness.

10
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Ron Morris, scout master of troop 494 in Olathe, Kansas, wrote in and said, love the podcast.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
He said, with regards to how many listeners you have,

12
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
you should probably consider those who copy and share the MP3 files with other leaders.

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Oh, I know people do that every once in a while, Ron.

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Ron says he's giving copies to his assistant scout masters.

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Well, thanks, Ron. Appreciate it.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
He did have a problem being able to download a couple of the podcasts when we went in and fixed that.

17
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Uh, thanks for being my eyes and ears and my quality control people, Ron and everyone else.

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
If you find a problem, yeah, do get in touch. Let me know.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
You get in touch by emailing me at scoutmastercg at Verizon.net.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Or you can go to scoutmastercg.com.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
We got a contact form and all kinds of fancy schmancy stuff over there.

22
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So the newsletter went out this week, the June edition of the newsletter,

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and the theme in this newsletter is the patrol method.

24
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
And the giveaway in this newsletter is several copies of working the patrol method,

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
which is a really transformative book and it comes with a very high recommendations.

26
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
And it will really aid you if you're a scoutmaster, assistant scoutmaster,

27
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
or you work with a Boy Scout group. Great book to have.

28
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
So get over to scoutmastercg.com and make sure you're subscribed to the newsletter

29
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
so you can get future newsletters, future giveaways.

30
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
So really interesting thing happened this week.

31
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
I had a package in the mail and that package came from a scouter in Pittsfield, Mass, Bill McFarland.

32
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
And Bill has this really interesting thing going on.

33
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
If you're old school scouting and you go back probably a little farther than you'd like to admit,

34
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
you'll remember in Boy's Life magazine there was a column called Whittling Gym.

35
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And Whittling Gym had a necrochip slide in it every month.

36
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And if you got your pen knife and a piece of wood and the column,

37
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
you could carve yourself a necrochip slide.

38
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
He always had some kind of cool, interesting idea.

39
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Well, Bill got the idea that he would go through

40
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
and he would carve all of the Whittling Gym necrochip slides.

41
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
And he's keeping track of that on a blog called Channeling Whittlin Gym

42
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
and it's channelingwhittlingym.blogspot.com

43
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
and there'll be a link to Bill's blog in the post that contains this podcast.

44
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Now, so I opened up the package and what is it?

45
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Well, it's a necrochip slide that Bill has carved

46
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
that is kind of the logo that I've been using for the past five or six years

47
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
on the podcast and on the blog of the saluting scout master.

48
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
And boy, what a nice gift.

49
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,000
There's a post about making it over at channelingwhittlingym.blogspot.com

50
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
and some very kind words from Bill and I just wanted to recognize that

51
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
and I was very touched, very nice gift and I really do appreciate it, Bill.

52
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
In other things going on, let's see here.

53
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
I got a book called Primitive Paradise by David Disselhorst

54
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
and I've just started reading it, got a good skim through it and everything like that

55
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
and what it's about is it's about scout camping in the United States

56
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
and it's actually a pretty interesting piece of work.

57
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
It centers mostly on one scout camp.

58
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The author, David Disselhorst, uses the experiences of this one scout camp

59
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
to kind of ponder the bigger issues about scout camping

60
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and the way it's handled and what its future is.

61
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,000
I think it's a worthy book.

62
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Hopefully we'll get to talk to David here on the podcast at one point

63
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
but I wanted to let you know about the book

64
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and there's a link to it in the post that contains this podcast.

65
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
So this time around, scout master ship in seven minutes or less,

66
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
we're going to talk a little bit about summer camp

67
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and we do have a comment that came through on the blog

68
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
to talk about in our listener answer section that we have here

69
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
and that's enough for any good podcast, don't you think?

70
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Hey, I do. So let's get started.

71
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Scout master ship in seven minutes or less.

72
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:55,000
One of the most fun things I've gotten to do in scouting

73
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
was be on our camp staff

74
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and I was able to do that for about 12 years

75
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
as a director in different departments and as a camp director

76
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and so the advice I'm about to give you

77
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
is kind of based on watching hundreds of different troops

78
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
and thousands of different scout leaders over those 12 years

79
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
come and experience a week of summer camp.

80
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
There's some common difficulties and problems that arise

81
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
and there's some things that you can do

82
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
that will just make things go a lot better for you

83
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
while you're there at camp with your scouts.

84
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Now scout camp is a great place to go if you're a scout.

85
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
It's fun, there's lots of challenges, there's great stuff to do

86
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
and it's the same as when you're an adult.

87
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
It's just a little bit of a different level.

88
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Now if it's the first couple of times you go to scout camp

89
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
it can be very stressful, you can be faced with a lot of new things

90
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
a lot of things that you're unfamiliar with

91
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
you're not quite sure what the expectations are

92
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
so you do have to take real good care of yourself.

93
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Now that's if you've been going to camp

94
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
if this year is your first time or your 25th year going

95
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
you have to take good care of yourself physically

96
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
because if you take good care of yourself physically

97
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
you're going to be in good shape mentally to handle camp.

98
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Our camp is one of the only remaining ones

99
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
that has a seven day schedule.

100
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
We bring everybody in on Sunday

101
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
and they leave on the following Sunday.

102
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
So somebody gets to camp you know

103
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
your average adult leader gets to camp on a Sunday afternoon

104
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
and the first day of camp that's kind of stressful

105
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
there's a lot of things to do, there's new things to learn

106
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
there's people to meet, places to get to

107
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and you might feel a little bit rushed

108
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and by the time you're headed off to bed that night

109
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,000
you're probably pretty tired

110
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
and you may sleep well and you may not

111
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
and you know the food's going to be a little different

112
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
the water's going to be a little different

113
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
your latrine's going to be a latrine

114
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
you're going to have a lot of the same experiences

115
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
that the boys do with unfamiliar stressors

116
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
and things like that

117
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
but you're also going to be older than they are

118
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
I don't know if you notice that

119
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
so you know your abilities are going to be

120
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
compromised maybe by being a bit older than they are

121
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
being a little more set in your ways

122
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and you could become cranky real fast

123
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and how do I know this?

124
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Do I have to tell you?

125
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
I mean I've been there

126
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,000
I've done it

127
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
I've not taken very good care of myself physically

128
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
and then I get real cranky

129
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and yeah then things just get ugly

130
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,000
so if you are hot, tired, hungry, thirsty

131
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
you need to get cool, you need to get rested

132
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
you need to get fed and you need to get hydrated

133
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
and that's going to be a pretty big service to you

134
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
yourself personally

135
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
your fellow leaders, your troop and the camp staff

136
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
if you're not cranky and mean

137
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
it's going to make the whole week go a lot easier

138
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
so that's one key piece of advice

139
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
you're coming into a situation

140
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
that you may not be familiar with

141
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
but you've got a staff there

142
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
people who are adults

143
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
and who've been at it for a long time

144
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
and then you've got young adults

145
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
guys who are around college age

146
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
who got a lot of energy

147
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and may have been at the camp staff job for several years

148
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and then you've got a bunch of younger staff members

149
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
who are basically learning right along with your scouts

150
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
they're learning a lot of things right along with your scouts

151
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
now they probably are a little bit farther ahead of them

152
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
as far as the camp things go

153
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
but maturity-wise and things like that

154
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:37,000
they're going to be more like your average 15, 16, 17-year-old

155
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and you've got to take that into account

156
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
you need to be considerate and supportive of them

157
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
in the same way that you're considerate

158
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and supportive of your scouts

159
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
you never ever want to step in

160
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and be raid or criticize or argue

161
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
with a camp staff member

162
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
you don't want to do that with a youth leader in your troop

163
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
you don't want to do that with a fellow adult leader

164
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and you certainly don't want to do that

165
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
with the presence of scouts

166
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
and now how much you may think

167
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
that that staff member deserves it

168
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
what you want to do is you want to have

169
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
either a quiet talk with them

170
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
or their boss

171
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
at an appropriate distance from the scouts

172
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
and that will usually solve the problem

173
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
and sometimes that might be your problem

174
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
you have to realize that camp staff members

175
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
are totally dedicated to making your stay worthwhile

176
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and your idea of what might be right

177
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
and their idea of what might be right

178
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
could be two different things

179
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
so you kind of got to go with the flow a little bit

180
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
you got to join in the program

181
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and encourage your scouts to do so

182
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
even if the program in your humble opinion

183
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
isn't all that great

184
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
if you go to a scout camp

185
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
and you're eating a dining hall

186
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
there's usually a program around the dining hall time

187
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
and you know it may be

188
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
a really great whiz bang

189
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
high energy program

190
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
it might not be all that great

191
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
it encourages a lackluster

192
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
kind of difficult program as a lively audience

193
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
a lively audience goes a long, long way

194
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and you and your troop

195
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
with the right attitude could be the catalyst

196
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
that makes for a great week for everybody in camp

197
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
you know in the vast scheme of things

198
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
I know that

199
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,000
as a staff member and as a director at camp

200
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
we always emphasize that we were serving scouts

201
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and scout leaders

202
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and we were doing our dangest

203
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
to make them comfortable

204
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and to make things work well for them

205
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
now that's being said

206
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
you don't want to go pulling rank

207
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
as a adult or a scout leader

208
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
you don't want to ask for special privileges

209
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
you don't want to try and impress the staff

210
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
members with your self importance

211
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
a lot of people

212
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
instantly respect your position

213
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
as a scout leader

214
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
and they're going to

215
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
try and do their best for you

216
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
just don't make a big giant deal out of it

217
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
there's really no rank to be pulled there

218
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
and remember that these guys are working hard

219
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
trying to help you

220
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
so just go easy

221
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
you need to look for the

222
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
opportunities to participate

223
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
with a lot of gusto

224
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and a lot of spirit in ceremonies

225
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and traditions and practices at camp

226
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
even if they're unfamiliar to you

227
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
if it's the first time you end up at a scout camp

228
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
you know maybe it's a new one

229
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
that you're trying out with your troop

230
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
get into it

231
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
jump right in

232
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
camps and camp staff members

233
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
are very proud of their traditions

234
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and you don't want to try to impose the way that

235
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
everybody did it at the last camp you were at

236
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
or the way that you think it should be done

237
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
let them call the shots

238
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
let them run the show

239
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and just jump in and have a great time with it

240
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
you'll be a camp with a lot of different scout troops

241
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
who will be run one way

242
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
and you know you might be doing things

243
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
in a different way

244
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and sometimes little inner troop rivalries

245
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
may develop

246
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
now scout age boys, as you know

247
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
they're pretty hyper competitive

248
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and they need to be reminded

249
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
about fairness and courtesy and sportsmanship

250
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
every once in a while

251
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
so you want to set the tone

252
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
when there's competition

253
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
of it being a gentlemanly scout like competition

254
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and you want to make sure that you require that of your scouts

255
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
as always

256
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
with anything we do in scouting

257
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
give our youth leaders all the responsibility

258
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
for success

259
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
see that they understand what needs to be done

260
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
how and why and then observe them

261
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
from a respectful distance

262
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
check in with them every evening

263
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
ask lots of questions

264
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and put your ore in when you're asked for it

265
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
where it's a matter of safety

266
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
but otherwise let them run their troop

267
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
let them work with the camp program

268
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and the camp staff to do that

269
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
you can run a lot of interference by learning

270
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
what happens next for them

271
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
or if you have the experience

272
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
that you can share with them

273
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
but let them have all the responsibility

274
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and all the credit

275
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
for the success of their troop

276
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
you know, be proud of your scouts

277
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
encourage them and congratulate them

278
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
admire them, don't try to be one of them

279
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
because you know

280
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
you're too old for that now

281
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
there'll be some opportunities

282
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
when you can join in the fun

283
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
at more or less the boy level

284
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
jump in and have fun

285
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
but don't interfere

286
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
so those are a few things that are going to help you

287
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
when you get to Scout Camp this year

288
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
next time around we're going to talk a little bit more

289
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
we're going to talk about preparing parents for Scout Camp

290
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
we're going to talk about homesickness

291
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
and things like that

292
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
but we'll be doing that in future podcasts

293
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:51,000
e-mail

294
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
e-mail that is folks

295
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and here's an answer to one of your e-mails

296
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
there's a post on the blog this week

297
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
it got a lot of discussion

298
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
parents should force

299
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
encourage, cajole their boys

300
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
to stay in Scouts

301
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and we just talked about some of the broad underlying concepts

302
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
of what happens when boys are

303
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
the age that they are

304
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
you know, in that middle school

305
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
in those high school years

306
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
when things change

307
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
what signs we should be looking for and everything like that

308
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
one of the replies I thought was kind of interesting

309
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
to that discussion

310
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
was from Ann Hendricks in Griffin, Georgia

311
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
who has a boy who's a tenderfoot Scout

312
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and it says, we encourage him

313
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
we don't push him

314
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
but we've decided

315
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
that he's not going to be allowed

316
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
to get a driver's license

317
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
or a date until he becomes an Eagle Scout

318
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
what do you think?

319
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
well Ann, I don't know

320
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
it's kind of hard to say because

321
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I don't want to give advice to parents

322
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
about how to raise their children

323
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
I can give you advice about

324
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
how to deal with Scouts and things like that

325
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
I've been around long enough

326
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
that I've seen people try this plan

327
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
and I've seen it work

328
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and I've seen it not work so great

329
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
you're talking about a tenderfoot Scout

330
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
so he's going to be 11, 12, maybe 13

331
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
at the oldest

332
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
I would imagine

333
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and that 11 or 12 or 13 year old

334
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
is going to be a different person

335
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
when he's 14, 15 or 16

336
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
it's just part of growing up

337
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
they change quite a bit

338
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
their ideas change

339
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
what they like changes

340
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
and I don't think

341
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
I don't know if it's a legitimate thing

342
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
for parents to start out

343
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
with an 11 year old and say

344
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
well it's going to be thus and so

345
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
when you're 16 and expect

346
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
there not to be any mitigating circumstances

347
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
between now and then

348
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
then again, like I said, I've seen that plan work

349
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
I've seen people

350
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
who've created that rule

351
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and held that up

352
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and managed to work it

353
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and work it well

354
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
it has a lot to do with the personality of the boy

355
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
it has a lot to do with the personality

356
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
of the parents and the circumstances

357
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
they find themselves in

358
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
I will advise this

359
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
when you're raising an adolescent boy

360
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
flexibility

361
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
is a pretty important skill to have

362
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
it's a pretty important thing

363
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
you've got to be rigidly flexible

364
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
there are some things that you can give in on

365
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and some things that you can

366
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
work with

367
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
there are going to be some standards that you're going to have to uphold

368
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
but

369
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
so far as scouting goes

370
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
I would make sure that if that is

371
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
the way that you're going to manage it

372
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
that the scout master knows

373
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
you keep up with it

374
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and you help him

375
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
you help him make that goal

376
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
and you do that not by doing the work for him

377
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
but by being encouraging

378
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
by being supportive

379
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
and helping remind him that he has this goal

380
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
in front of him because

381
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
we all have set this up in such a way

382
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
that he owns the goal

383
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
that it's not something that you're imposing on him

384
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
but it's something that he owns

385
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
and that he agrees is a good idea

386
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
so I know there's probably a lot of

387
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
people listening who have some thoughts

388
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
on this particular question

389
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
I'd love to have you get in touch with me

390
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
and you're going to find out how to do that in just a moment

